---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import Container from "../components/ui/Container.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from "../utils/constants";
---

<MainLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What the Open Waters site and APIs collect, what they do not, and who operates them."
>
  <Container class="py-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="mb-8">Privacy</h1>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
        <p class="text-fg-muted text-xl leading-relaxed">
          Open Waters is run by Open Water Software, LLC. There are no accounts
          here, so there is no profile to build. This page describes what the
          site and the APIs actually see, and what they deliberately do not.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">The website</h2>

        <p>
          On our information and token pages, page views and selected actions
          are counted with <a href="https://plausible.io/">Plausible</a>, which
          is hosted in the EU and does not use cookies. We send the page
          category, an approved referring site, and a small set of approved
          campaign tags. Plausible also reports aggregate country, browser, and
          device information. No advertising or social network scripts load
          here.
        </p>

        <p>
          Actions include successful AIS token issuance, the token page
          observing recent receiver data, and opening selected project, setup,
          download, or email links. Opening a link does not tell us that you
          installed a tool, completed a download, or sent an email. We do not
          send tokens, keys, station or vessel identifiers, names, coordinates,
          form contents, or raw link destinations to analytics. Identifying page
          paths are grouped and other query parameters and fragments are
          removed. Analytics is not joined to your API credentials or later use
          of an external client.
        </p>

        <p>
          The site sets no cookies of its own. If you create an AIS token in the
          browser, the key is kept in your browser's <code>localStorage</code> on
          your own machine, and clearing site data removes it.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">The APIs</h2>

        <p>
          The tides API needs no authentication and asks for nothing about you.
          It counts requests per IP address to enforce its published rate
          limits, which is the only thing the address is used for.
        </p>

        <p>
          An AIS token is an Ed25519 keypair generated in your browser. The
          private key never leaves it; the service stores only the public key
          and the token's claims. No email address, name or payment detail is
          required or requested for the free tiers. A token may optionally be
          bound to an address range, in which case that range is part of the
          token. Commercial arrangements are made over email, so for those the
          only record is the correspondence itself.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">Vessel positions</h2>

        <p>
          AIS is a public radio broadcast, and the positions Open Waters serves
          are the ones vessels transmit for anyone to hear. Every event names
          the source it came from, whether that is a national open feed, an
          exchange, or a volunteer receiver. Volunteer receivers are identified
          by an opaque hash or by the station's own MMSI, not by the operator.
        </p>

        <p>
          The track endpoint, <code>/v1/vessels/&#123;mmsi&#125;/track</code>,
          serves the positions the network heard from one vessel; how far back a
          request reaches depends on the token it carries. If you contribute a
          receiver and want to stop, or want its history disassociated from it,
          email us and we will sort it out.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">Sharing</h2>

        <p>
          Nothing collected here is sold, and there is nothing collected here
          worth selling. Contributed AIS data is forwarded to the open exchanges
          named in the <a href="/api/ais/">AIS documentation</a>, which is the
          point of contributing it. The site and APIs run on Cloudflare, which
          sees request traffic as the infrastructure provider.
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-12 mb-6">Questions</h2>

        <p>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with anything
          about this page, including a request to remove something. A person reads
          it.
        </p>

        <p class="text-fg-faint text-sm">
          Open Water Software, LLC · <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}
            >{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
</MainLayout>
